WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.560
- If I ran Nintendo today, I'd
probably put out 5,000 pieces

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.020
of creative content
starring Zelda and Mario

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.030
and build up the IP and make
sure all those characters

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.090
and all those visions would be winning

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.330
on TikTok, Instagram, YouTube.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.980
I would just produce at scale.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.090
This has now become
singularly a making game.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:21.330
Make.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:22.180
Make, make, make.

00:00:23.393 --> 00:00:24.490
(yells)

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.021
You got your perspective.

00:00:26.021 --> 00:00:27.208
(engine whirring)

00:00:27.208 --> 00:00:29.421
(audience cheers)

00:00:29.421 --> 00:00:30.254
I just want to be happy.

00:00:30.254 --> 00:00:32.030
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.530
(jazzy music)

00:00:35.499 --> 00:00:36.430
- [Male Audience Member] If
you were to give a percentage

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:39.820
of your work, life and home-life
balance, what would it be?

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:41.250
Do you think the balance is important

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.470
to being successful in
your overall day to day?

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:44.630
- Yes.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:47.500
But I also think that
balance ebbs and flows.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:51.530
So, I'm probably spending
more time on my life

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:52.863
right now than ever.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:55.603
I mean, look.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.373
People look at things funny.

00:01:00.374 --> 00:01:02.850
Between being fully, all-in on weekends,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.060
between five other weeks of
vacation during the year,

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.370
you're already at a place
where you're talking about

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.023
you only work 225, of the 360,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:14.590
you know like, people are very funny

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.460
about how they think
about work-life balance.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.683
I think that for me,
somebody who enjoys work,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:26.040
between those vacations and weekends,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.460
and now my kids are
getting older so now it is,

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.670
go to a soccer match in
the middle of the day

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:32.280
which is super foreign to me.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.720
I used to be nine a.m.,
midnight, every minute, boom.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.740
So, but again I think it's similar

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.920
to not judging myself that
somebody's upset this morning.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.790
You can only do your best.

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.880
And I think one of the biggest
weaknesses in our society

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.960
is that people beat themselves
up at every, single second

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.350
instead of looking at
it in a year's worth,

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:56.620
or a life's work, or at
least a month's work.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.130
And so I think balance is important,

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.430
I also think everybody
here has different balance.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.490
Some people think nine
to five is balanced.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.600
I would hate my life if
I worked nine to five.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.490
It's just too much downtime for me.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.010
I enjoy what I do too much.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.170
So you get to define balance.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.830
You get to judge yourself.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.000
And people literally take
the opinions of their parents

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.330
and friends into how they
parent or work-life balance.

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:22.533
That is so laughable.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.510
Too much peer pressure.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.843
Too much judgment.

00:02:28.843 --> 00:02:31.093
(crunches)

00:02:33.508 --> 00:02:34.630
- [Male Audience Member] So in terms

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.850
of where you see advertising,

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:38.390
and maybe not even just digital,

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.090
where would you see advertise
going in five, 10 years?

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:44.083
- I have no idea.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:47.090
A hypothesis I have, definitely,

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.380
is Voice is gonna be a player.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:51.930
I definitely believe
that the new interface

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.440
that's brewing is Voice and
its impact will be as large

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.560
as the mobile device when at scale.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.890
15, 17 years from now.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.430
I'm not, you know I get a lot of credit

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:04.420
because of the way I invested

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:07.320
and all the documentation
of being right a lot.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.000
But what I try to remind people is,

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.800
when I was saying those things,
it had already happened.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:16.270
I'm not predicting TikTok,
it has 1.5 billion downloads.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.717
The fuck kind of guess is that?

00:03:17.717 --> 00:03:19.330
(audience laughs)

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.573
It's just that people love
to say "no" about today.

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.593
Let alone the future.

00:03:25.745 --> 00:03:30.260
I'm not worried about AR,
Blockchain, you know, ML, VR.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:31.520
All that's coming.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:32.940
I'm watching all of it.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.360
But no real people use it yet.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.350
And so people love to jump the gun

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:41.830
on technology advancements
ahead of the consumer behavior.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:45.500
I am blindly a day trader of this second.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.500
Before I worry about how to
get my AR capabilities down,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.830
why not maximize the shit out of today?

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:53.913
Right this second?

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:57.963
LinkedIn, TikTok, Instagram,
grossly under-priced.

00:03:59.399 --> 00:04:03.480
- And advertising's always been
the leading edge of culture.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:07.780
So AR has to, and VR has to
begin to weave its way in,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.010
and as advertising agencies,

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.360
do we have to be at the forefront on that?

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.840
- In a practical way.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.080
The reason I've been able to do that

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.130
is 'cause I'm willing to make less money.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.240
Not super complicated, back to CFO.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:21.817
CFO walks in says,

00:04:21.817 --> 00:04:23.850
"I want to shut down
the Voice department."

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.290
I'm like, "Then shut
down the whole company."

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:27.520
He's like, "But it's not profitable."

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.600
I'm like, "I'm aware."

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.800
That's the punchline.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.380
The reason most agencies
aren't able to pull it off

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.140
is because they don't want to
give up the margin that year.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.853
You're not gonna have clients
often pay to subsidize,

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.060
those new advancements.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.620
But I think the key is to
not be too far in advance.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:50.460
So we do have people
flirting with AR and VR

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.550
and things of that nature,
but it is flirting.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:54.460
Because it's just not close.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.910
I don't think people
realize how not close VR is.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:01.770
I don't know a single person
that knows a single person

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.513
that spends an hour a
day in a VR environment.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:08.490
Every person spends seven
hours a day on a phone.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:11.500
I'm talking about a real delta.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:12.803
So, yeah.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.320
To me it's the speed in adjusting

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.020
to what the consumer's done.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:19.840
What I'm proud of is,

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.950
as soon as Musically
gets bought by TikTok,

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:24.040
they relaunch, stuff starts happening.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:27.470
Our ability at Vayner to be
a creative on that depart,

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.670
in that world within
moments of it becoming real,

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:33.170
that's the strength more than predicting.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:36.680
It's the speed in which
you can execute in today

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.180
versus the variable of
being right about tomorrow.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.460
- [Host] We talked a little bit

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:46.320
before the live stream started
about how you've scaled

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.070
your company and just from
a cultural standpoint,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.350
but also from a process standpoint,

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.330
from an accountability standpoint.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:55.950
Could you speak that a little bit?

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.462
- Yeah, it's a human game.

00:05:57.462 --> 00:05:59.820
You know, from a cultural standpoint,

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:00.900
from a process standpoint,

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.202
it's just judging the judgers at my level.

00:06:04.202 --> 00:06:08.520
I think people are very naive
on how much the variable

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.030
of success is predicated on the CEO.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.950
She or he has a whole lot of say.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:14.410
They're the judge and the jury.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.513
A CEO is basically the Supreme Court.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.210
It's pretty final.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:20.930
These are dictatorships.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.990
Obviously there's boards in some companies

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.030
that may have a little juice,

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:28.190
but the reality is that
the way we've done it

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:30.133
is by actually caring about it.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:32.130
It's not super complicated.

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:34.930
I would, we're far more advanced
on culture than process.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:37.840
I hate process so much that
I don't look at it often

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:39.120
and any time I do look at it,

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.350
it look like it's going really poorly

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:44.186
and so then I just cry and go to sleep.

00:06:44.186 --> 00:06:45.019
(laughs)

00:06:45.019 --> 00:06:46.170
And so.

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:47.610
- You have a Chief Heart Officer.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:48.443
- We do.

00:06:48.443 --> 00:06:49.360
- Do you have a Chief Process Officer?

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:50.193
- We don't.

00:06:50.193 --> 00:06:52.483
And I think that there's a couple reasons.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:54.990
And we won't.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.140
I believe that human beings
that work in companies

00:06:57.140 --> 00:06:59.390
are disproportionately
obsessed with process.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.400
And I think the second you
start putting the process

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:05.540
in front of the output you lose

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:07.200
and I think almost every company

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.090
puts the process in front of the output.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:14.360
I'd rather the process
be murky and breakable

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:15.713
than put on a pedestal.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.770
It's a tricky one.
- It is.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.880
- It's a tricky one but if you
listen to what I just said,

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.860
what you'll learn is it
will come down to casting.

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:28.210
And if you work on culture

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:31.100
and people aren't political
with each other and jerks,

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:35.530
they'll figure it out much
easier and with less friction

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:39.930
but the reality is most
agencies have too much process

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.500
and way too much insecurity in politics

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:45.227
and thus it's slow and clunky and crappy.

00:07:45.227 --> 00:07:46.060
- [Female Audience Member]
Are you saying that

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.470
data privacy regulations
are ready to handle

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:50.904
a world in which everything is

00:07:50.904 --> 00:07:51.820
AI that we do?

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:52.960
- So I think, I think.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.170
I'll answer the second one first.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:57.000
It's about data privacy
regulations can handle a world

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.410
where AI, where every,
single product is smart,

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:02.210
at scale, two decades from now,

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:05.540
which is the assumption or
guess that I have right now.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:07.960
No, but I think there's a
much bigger conversation

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:10.290
around data privacy that's not being had,

00:08:10.290 --> 00:08:11.930
which is, I actually
don't think people care

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:12.830
about the privacy.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.400
I think there's a much more
thoughtful conversation going on

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.580
that will eventually play out,

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.740
which is, I'm gonna predict

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.370
that it's gonna go the other way.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:26.880
I would prefer that every technology

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.130
knew everything about me.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:30.213
I mean it.

00:08:30.213 --> 00:08:33.060
Unless you kill my
children or steal my money,

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:34.920
I prefer you know everything.

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:36.940
This notion of privacy.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:39.090
What privacy are we
actually talking about?

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.820
What websites I went on?

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:42.810
My credit card number?

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:46.120
You give away your credit card
number every day, everywhere.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.693
Target and the IRS have much
bigger breaches than Facebook.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:55.360
I think there's this
fun narrative right now

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:57.240
to demonize technology

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.570
because we don't like being accountable.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:02.920
The Russians didn't make
you vote for someone.

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.000
You did.
(audience laughs)

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.390
You decided to believe.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:07.810
And so this huge lack of
accountability will play out.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:08.697
So, I'm not.

00:09:08.697 --> 00:09:09.750
You know, look.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.350
If somebody is creeped out

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:14.020
that the refrigerator
reordered their milk,

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.800
'cause the milk spoke to the frigerator

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.520
and it said, "We have one pour left,"

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:18.770
they're more than welcome to not.

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:20.117
I keep reminding people, I'm like,

00:09:20.117 --> 00:09:22.067
"Don't have a smart phone."

00:09:22.067 --> 00:09:23.930
"Gary this all sucks, it's all terrible."

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.837
I'm like, "Delete Facebook."

00:09:27.867 --> 00:09:28.710
"I did."

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.147
I'm like, "But you're on Instagram."

00:09:31.147 --> 00:09:32.599
"Yeah, but I like it."

00:09:32.599 --> 00:09:34.390
(audience laughs)

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:35.223
I mean.

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:39.097
Look, I think as an agency
when you think about

00:09:39.097 --> 00:09:41.370
the smartification, you're
talking about building.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:43.010
You're talking about actually being indev.

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:45.030
Which is not a big passion
of mine, ironically.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.070
I don't like building the highways,

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.570
I like driving them
better than anybody else.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:52.540
So I think when you talk
about smartification,

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.830
where it's all gonna lead to is brand.

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:56.510
Brand is gonna become
the most important thing

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:59.030
because when you're
ordering on a voice device,

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:02.060
you better say, "Kleenex" not "tissues."

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.210
You'd better say "Nike" not "sneakers."

00:10:04.210 --> 00:10:08.100
Because then Amazon or Google
are gonna pick for you, right.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:09.820
Same thing with brand.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:12.700
Once I put that Pepsi in that fridge,

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:14.570
that fridge is reordering Pepsi,

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:18.140
not me deciding to pick up
Coke at an end cap at Walmart.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:19.437
So, brand.

00:10:19.437 --> 00:10:22.600
It's like I can't yell about it enough.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.130
Look at my own behaviors.

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:26.610
The signature, the sonic branding.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.260
Like this is all branding for me.

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:32.140
All my friends call me
a sucker for giving away

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:34.300
the 270 page deck the other day.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:35.380
Not me.

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:36.800
I think about brand.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.060
You feel warmer towards me.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:40.010
I did something nice for you.

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:41.120
People don't get it.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:44.363
So, that's how I see, I
think privacy's really funny.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.747
I think Europe is a really miss...

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.980
Is really misplaying the world.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:57.980
I think Europe's growing
irrelevance has a lot to do

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:01.240
with the defense they have
around all this stuff.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:02.210
When Google and Facebook

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.860
and all these other companies
don't want to be there,

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.510
that's going to be bad, not good.

00:11:08.521 --> 00:11:09.354
- [Female Audience Member] How do you,

00:11:09.354 --> 00:11:11.180
you're obviously putting
out a lot of content

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.640
over so many different platforms,

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:17.070
so my question is how do you
measure the success of that?

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.140
Is it based on purely likes

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.530
and as we start to see likes going away.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:25.050
- Likes will go away only
for our insecurities outward,

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.780
but we will still see them on the backend.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:27.785
But keep going, is that?

00:11:27.785 --> 00:11:28.948
- [Female Audience Member]
Yeah, so I'm wondering

00:11:28.948 --> 00:11:32.130
what is your way of measuring?

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:35.630
- My measurement on success
is far more qualitative

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:36.730
'cause I'm trying to win on brand.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.240
I'm not trying to sell K-Swiss sneakers.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.110
I know that I sold a
thousand pairs yesterday

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.040
on this singular, Instagram post,

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.950
but that is not where my mind
goes on, is that good or bad.

00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:47.610
My mind works this way.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:48.960
When I went to the airport last week

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:51.860
and a seven year old ran up
to me and asked for a selfie,

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:53.313
I knew TikTok was working.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.650
So for me it's qualitative.

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:57.970
I know something's working

00:11:57.970 --> 00:11:59.963
based on reading the comments at scale.

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:04.100
So for me it's much more
the comments themselves.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.100
Not how many comments,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:09.900
the interpretation of the
intent of the comments.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.510
'Cause I'm building brand,

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:13.367
I'm not worried about how
many likes and shares it gets.

